Pairwise independence does not imply mutual independence. Tossing a fair coin twice gives $Omega = {(0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1)}$ with $PP({omega}) = 1/4$ for each outcome. The events
$
  A & = {(0,0), (0,1)} \
  B & = {(0,0), (1,0)} \
  C & = {(1,0), (0,1)}
$
satisfy $PP(A) = PP(B) = PP(C) = 1/2$ and $PP(A inter B) = PP(A inter C) = PP(B inter C) = 1/4$, so they are pairwise independent. But $PP(A inter B inter C) = PP(emptyset) = 0 != 1/8$, so they are not mutually independent.
